amiliar refrain David Lammy  the MP for Tottenham who spent his  childhood playing in Broadwater Farm  one of the few trusted politicians a lot  time in here is quoted in happier number  28 I think my cousin's lived at justjust  up there on the top and I very much live  between their house and my house which  was just sort of parallel to this road  down here and we grew up together  do you think that things have got better  for the communities that lived on  interstates when you growing up no I  don't actually my afraid I don't think  things have got better because what  there was in the 1970s particularly was  full employment you could get a job that  was quite well-paid and you could  survive David Lammy says he understands  London's housing divides more than most  he lost a friend at Granville Tower and  is furious about the conditions many in  the capital have to enjoy fire doors  that aren't fixed is a big concern now  in many communities mistrust of the  political class runs deep if there is  any suspicion of a cover-up at gruntal  tower that will only get worse so it's  been a little over two weeks since the  ground-floor Tower tragedy you've been  talking a lot to different communities  living in similar tower blocks how have  they been reacting my sense is that if  you live in a tower block a state in  Britain in fact if you live in an estate  there's quite a lot of fear particularly  amongst the women the mothers with  children that I talk to and that fear is  linked to their their now very  questioning of the stay-put advice  worried about how they would make their  way out with their kids at two three  o'clock in the morning  and their untrusting of if you like the  authority figures that they're reliant  on to give them the best advice the  local authority the housing management  organization even the fire safety and  that's partly to do with the divide  that's open up in society the folk that  turn up to give you that advice so not  people who were living in a tower block  or even know anyone living in a tower  block it would seem so  we're not in a great place we're not in  a great place in Britain you are someone  who isn't in that camp because you of  course grew up around this estate here  you lost a friend in the Granville Tower  tragedy you must still be grieving I'm  still in shock in a sense where we don't  you know when you've when a young woman  it's just beginning her life you know no  one can brilliantly believe that she's  not here there's no way of making smoke  in your lungs or burning presentable  this it's it's horrific you touched on  this a bit earlier but do you think  people trust the authorities to get to  the bottom of what went wrong and to try  and put it right I think the  overwhelming sense from the victims is  that this is a crime that something went  so badly wrong here that it amounts to  gross negligence manslaughter  so people are hanging on that criminal  investigation and one updates about how  that's developing we've heard that a lot  of police are on the commune Oh involved  in the criminal investigation which is  good we don't know where the documents  have been seized  we don't know who's been spoken to but  that's the importance and then in  relation to the inquiry the inquiry is  about yes getting to the truth but then  also making recommendations so that can  never happen again but that's years  years away and then as always it's up to  the government of the day to act on  those recommendations and there'll be  years off by then the news agenda will  have moved on the scrutiny will have  moved on I won't have moved on and  parliamentarians likely won't have move  so our job will be to hold to account to  make sure that there is justice for  those victims but that this never  happens again  talking about that inquiry the judge  himself has said that he's doubtful that  it will be as wide-ranging that some  people hope are you worried about what  happens with that inquiring I'm  disappointing that he's expressed doubt  of that kind before even meeting the  survivors and victims this is not a  court case there's not two sides there  are survivors and victims and it seems  to me if you're leading an inquiry  you're walking with them to get to the  truth and you need to be looking at the  terms of reference and the scope of your  inquiry in partnership with those people  so I'm worried but he's kicked off the  inquiry framing it in a sort of small  tiny way and do you think he's the right  person to lead the inquiry look  politicians have to always be careful of  being in a critical place in relation to  the judiciary but he is a white  upper-middle-class man who I suspect has  never ever visited a tower block housing  estate and certainly hasn't slept  tonight on the 20th floor of lawn I hope  he will do that in the days ahead the  job is not just to be independent and  judicious I'm sure he's eminently  legally qualified of course he is it is  also to be empathetic and to work with  these people on this journey and to sit  with them and understand that their  lives were in the hands of the state and  something badly badly failed it's a  shame that we couldn't find a woman to  lead the inquiry or indeed an ethnic  minority to lead the inquiry in 2017 and  I think the victims will also say to  themselves when push comes to shove  there are some powerful people here  contractors subcontractors local  authorities governments and they look  like this judge  whose side will he be on so he needs to  get close to those victims and survivors  very very quickly and established that  he is after the truth and he's fearless  and independent and he won't be swayed  because he too is a part of the  establishment talking about walking with  victims what do you make of the  political responses on both the council  level but also the national government  level have politicians done enough to  walk with the victims well to lose  everything  sometimes the lives of other family  members but literally everything you own  to go up in flames is devastating for  any human soul but then to find that  you're not cradled and supported after  that I'm afraid as a shocking indictment  of our country and so I have to say that  we have failed as a nation and I I'm not  going to make that party political we  failed to support those people and I'm  now going to get emotional again but  that makes me very upset very upset that  we that we did that in those early days  and still today something has badly  badly got wrong on wrong in that local  authority who represents those people  but the gap between the people that  represents that is huge  now some have said that you have  suggested that there's been a cover-up  on the number of people who've died in  the ground for terror a disaster what  you said was that what people say is  that if you put the numbers out early  there could be civil unrest were you  really saying that the number of people  dead could have been covered up to  prevent riots the police have filled in  a little bit of the information we now  know there are 23 homes they don't know  how many people were in them and they  don't actually know who owned the home  leased the home ran the home so we know  a little bit more but we still don't  have a list of survivors we're not able  to subtract the amount of survivors from  the approximate number of people in the  building  we know that 80 people have lost their  lives but the view amongst the victims I  spoke to them yesterday is that many  more lost their lives and you have to  get close to these people to understand  the degree of trust and how badly they  have been let down and therefore I  believe the way to deal with that is  constant communication the communication  has been poor it's got a little bit  better in the last day or two but it's  been poor hitherto to this point  Jonathan or the shadow Chancellor said  that the victims were murdered what I  murdered no I think that's not the right  language for me the right language is  this amounts it seems to me to corporate  manslaughter or gross negligence  manslaughter Jeremy Corbyn who's also  said that what happened at Grenville  exposed the disastrous effects of  austerity was that right bearing in mind  that a lot of this cladding was put up  under a Labour government the way I talk  about it is a tale of two cities  austerity and here in London you see it  writ large but you see it in other major  cities across the country you see at one  end of the spectrum social housing a  lack of quality and indifference to  people's needs and concerns and at the  other end of London huge gentrification  huge cranes huge buildings huge resource  and you absolutely see that in the  London Borough of Kensington and Chelsea  so yes it is a tale of austerity but  it's also a tale of massive massive  growth and they are sitting deeply  divided it seems to me in Britain at  this point in time we do this interview  from the Broadwater it's taken Tottenham  for many people it's a symbol of those  divisions that you were talking about do  you think that what happened at  Granville has perhaps  exposed brought lights at some of those  splits between communities that were  here in London already and also in other  parts of the country I think at the back  end of the election and one of the  reasons why the Labour Party confounded  expectations and did a lot better on the  Jeremy Corbyn and people expected was  that actually the public were up for a  discussion about public services about  the limits of the welfare state what  we've stripped back and I think Glen  feel then happened and brings out right  to the public for its really gotten to  the heart of the Britain that I know a  Britain that likes to see itself as fair  as tolerant and and therefore is  uncomfortable with with part of the face  it's seeing back in the mirror of itself  how people in these communities been  listened to before they've not been  listening to they've not been listened  to for years and that I'm afraid that  it's not just about the last 7 years of  austerity that goes beyond party  politics hearing what they're saying  about the quality of their homes hearing  what they're saying about the kind of  support they need hearing what they're  saying about the cap on their earnings  in relation to their the jobs and their  expectations we've got very very poor at  that so poor that when people are  desperate as they are in the case of  Glenfield the way they've been treated  it's horrendous
 

